segments.en:
This brief but dense halacha maps Chol HaMoed onto the concept of lashon Targum &mdash; the Aramaic translation-language that stands between Lashon HaKodesh (the Holy Tongue = Yom Tov = the talmid chacham who has already won the battle) and the seventy languages of the nations (the weekday = the dominion of the Sitra Achara). Chol HaMoed is Nogah &mdash; the intermediate realm where good and evil are intermixed, where the essential battle and test takes place.

From this, Reb Nosson derives: why some labors are forbidden and some permitted (because it is mixed of weekday and festival); why the matter was entrusted to the Sages (because it is the aspect of the Oral Torah, which is itself the aspect of Targum &mdash; the discernment of permitted and forbidden, kasher and pasul); why Chol HaMoed falls between the first and last days of Yom Tov (like the Baal Shem Tov&rsquo;s parable of teaching a child to walk: first you hold him, then you let go, then you catch him again); and why disgracing Chol HaMoed is linked to breaching the Bris &mdash; because the essential guarding of the covenant takes place precisely in the realm of Targum/Nogah, where the battle is fought.

&#10042; Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman &#10042;
